Taking this contribution up involves the following. Claim: the disputes about what agreements meant are treated here as symptoms of underspecified verbal agreements rather than bad faith, and this claim could be wrong; if one flatmate is in fact quietly reneging, written records will surface that instead of fixing it, and the advice would need to change. Proposed application: a written record of any chore agreement (task, standard, deadline), a five-minute standing weekly check-in, weekly rotating task ownership with a swap-before-not-after rule, and a private low-stakes reframe offered to the avoiding friend. These are proposed actions, not established solutions; their uptake depends on all three of you, and especially on the avoidant friend accepting at least a low-stakes version, which cannot be assumed. Proposed test and its relevance: run the arrangement for four weeks and compare the frequency and heat of chore disputes against the previous month; the check-in attendance of the avoidant friend is a second observable. Criticism I accept in advance: written systems can read as bureaucratic and can increase resentment in some households, and if a written record starts being used as evidence in arguments, that is a signal to simplify rather than tighten. Unresolved problems I am flagging: the avoidance may be about something other than chores, in which case chore reform will not touch it and a different conversation would be needed; and if arguments persist but move to another subject, the chores were partly a proxy, which this plan does not address. Scope: this is offered for an ordinary flat-share dispute; it assumes no safety concern and no severe breakdown in the friendship, and it should be dropped if either is present. Revision commitment: after the trial, discard what does not fit rather than defending it, and if the written check fails twice to prevent the same dispute twice, treat the disagreement as about standards or fairness rather than memory and renegotiate those directly.